sec.p:
Four studies (Wilding et al., 2021; Wadden et al., 2021; Rubino et al., 2022; O'Neil et al., 2018
), including 3,612 individuals, noted a change in CRP, a marker of inflammation. The analysis result demonstrated that semaglutide achieved a greater decrease from baseline in CRP than placebo, with statistically significant differences (MD: −1.09 mg/L; 95% CI: −1.50 to −0.69; 
p
 ˂ 0.00001), as shown in Figure 9 and Supplementary Table S3. Five studies (Wilding et al., 2021; Wadden et al., 2021; Rubino et al., 2021; Rubino et al., 2022; O'Neil et al., 2018), including 4,415 individuals, reported changes in TC, HDL, LDL, VLDL, and TG. Four studies (Rubino et al., 2021; Wadden et al., 2021; Wilding et al., 2021; Rubino et al., 2022), including 3,554 individuals, reported a change in FFA. Meta-analyses confirmed that semaglutide was accompanied by numeric improvements in these lipid-related indicators compared with placebo, with the exception of HDL (Figure 10, Supplementary Figures S9–S13, Supplementary Table S3). The corresponding funnel plots were also basically symmetric without publication bias (Supplementary Figure S8C, Supplementary Figure S14).
